  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nOur active, moist weather pattern continues as we remain \nsandwiched between a closed low over Northern CA and the \nsubtropical ridge centered near Little Rock. SPC analysis shows\nabout 1.2\" of PW, so heavy rain remains a threat. A weak s/w is\nover the Northern Gulf of California, and that will be our main\nplayer this evening into the overnight hours as it lifts north\nthrough the region. Prior to then, convection will be a little\ndelayed and limited to the mountains. By around 6PM, a complex of\nstorms will start moving in from Mexico associated with the low\nover the Gulf of California. The main precip threat associated\nwith this system will be across the Rio Grande Valley, which\nreceived a lot of rainfall last night. Thus, the current FFA looks\ngood. \n\nThe aforementioned low will push into Northern NM by Friday\nafternoon while we remain under the influence of a trough. Another\nlittle s/w trough axis will swing through Friday evening, but this\ntime the focus for storms will mainly be east of the Rio Grande.\nAnother trough axis will swing through the Parent low over\nYellowstone Saturday afternoon, but the focus will be near the\neastern border of our CWA. \n\nA s/w ridge will fill in behind the upper level trough/low with\nfair and warm weather for Sunday. Our CWA is expected to be precip\nfree. Monday will be a repeat of Sunday, but by Tuesday, moist,\nsubtropical southerly flow will reestablish itself over NM and Far\nWest Texas. Tuesday and into the rest of next week looks quite\nactive as systems ride up the moist, southerly flow.",
